TITLE:: SDGSampleFeatures
summary:: what a sample sounds like, as numbers: envelope, onsets, tempo, key, in the language
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SDGSampleTags, Classes/SDGSampleEdit, Classes/SDGSlicer, Classes/SDGSampleAnalysis

DESCRIPTION::
The analysis behind the tags, the editor's grid and the slicer. A file (or the
Signals the editor holds) is mixed to mono and brought down to about 22 kHz,
then looked at in two short-time spectra and a fine envelope. Nothing needs the
server or a plugin.

code::
f = SDGSampleFeatures.analyse(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav");
f[\bpm]; f[\bpmSure]; f[\key]; f[\keySure];
f[\attack]; f[\length]; f[\onsets];
SDGSampleFeatures.pickOnsets(f[\peaks], 0.8);    // more attacks
SDGSampleFeatures.parseKey("fsm");               // (tonic: 6, mode: minor)
::

table::
## strong::envelope:: || attack (10% to 90% of the peak), decay (to 20 dB under), length (to the last point within 20 dB), audible (within 40 dB), sustain (the share within 15 dB), crest, start
## strong::spectrum:: || 1024-point frames every 256 samples: centroid, flatness and the median flatness, the share under 150 Hz and over 5 kHz, and sweep - how far the strongest bin under 400 Hz falls after the loudest frame
## strong::onsets:: || the spectral flux of the log magnitude against the frame two back; every peak of it with its salience, how far it stands over the median of the half second around it
## strong::tempo:: || only with four attacks and a second of sound. The autocorrelation of the flux against the frame before, scored as a comb (a beat, two, three, four) over 60 .. 200 bpm with a mild preference round 120. A bpm that fits the file's length in whole beats wins when it scores within 10%. Sure when it stands out and nothing unrelated comes close. The beat offset puts a beat where the sound starts unless a grid elsewhere lands on ten times as much
## strong::key:: || the peaks of 4096-point spectra, 55 Hz .. 4 kHz, at their interpolated frequencies, the file's tuning taken out, folded into 12 pitch classes, matched against the Krumhansl-Kessler profiles. A one-shot mostly on one pitch class is a note, with the octave of its lowest strong partial. A sound without enough pitch has no key
::

A 30-second loop takes about half a second; a drum hit a few milliseconds.
Called inside a Routine with link::#*breathing:: set, every long loop gives the
rest of sclang a turn every link::#*sliceSeconds::, so a background analysis never
holds up a pattern for longer than that.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: analyse
ARGUMENT:: path
a sound file.
ARGUMENT:: seconds
how much of it to read; nil for link::#*maxSeconds::.
RETURNS:: an Event, see link::#*analyseMono::, with the file's code::sampleRate::,
code::numChannels::, code::numFrames::, code::duration::, code::partial:: and
code::version::; nil when it cannot be read.

METHOD:: analyseChannels
The same from one Signal per channel, as link::Classes/SDGSampleEdit:: holds them.
ARGUMENT:: channels
ARGUMENT:: sampleRate
ARGUMENT:: seconds
nil for all of it.

METHOD:: analyseMono
ARGUMENT:: mono
a Signal.
ARGUMENT:: rate
its sample rate.
ARGUMENT:: duration
the whole file's, for the tempo's length fit.
RETURNS:: an Event: code::peak::, code::rms::, code::attack::, code::decay::,
code::sustain::, code::audible::, code::length::, code::start::, code::crest::,
code::centroid::, code::flatness::, code::flatnessMedian::, code::low::,
code::high::, code::sweep::, code::odf:: and code::beatCurve:: (the two flux
curves), code::hopSeconds::, code::peaks::, code::onsets:: (at sensitivity 0.5),
code::bpm::, code::bpmConfidence::, code::bpmSure::, code::beatOffset::,
code::lengthFit::, code::chroma::, code::tonal::, code::key::, code::tonic::,
code::mode:: (code::\major::, code::\minor:: or code::\note::), code::octave::,
code::keyConfidence::, code::keySure::.

METHOD:: read
METHOD:: fromChannels
The mono signal at about link::#*targetRate::, and the file's numbers.

METHOD:: envelope
METHOD:: spectra
METHOD:: onsetPeaks
METHOD:: tempo
METHOD:: beatPhase
METHOD:: chroma
METHOD:: key
The stages of link::#*analyseMono::, each usable on its own.

METHOD:: pickOnsets
The onset times whose salience clears what a sensitivity asks; of two closer
than code::minGap::, the stronger.
ARGUMENT:: peaks
code::[[seconds, salience] ...]::, as link::#*onsetPeaks:: answers.
ARGUMENT:: sensitivity
0 keeps only the strongest attacks, 1 nearly every bump.
ARGUMENT:: minGap
seconds.
RETURNS:: the times, seconds.

METHOD:: thresholdFor
ARGUMENT:: sensitivity
RETURNS:: the salience an onset needs: 0.6 at 0 down to 0.01 at 1, on a log scale.

METHOD:: parseKey
ARGUMENT:: str
code::"Am"::, code::"F#"::, code::"Eb"::, code::"C3"::, or Tidal's spelling,
code::"fsm"::, code::"bf"::.
RETURNS:: code::(tonic: 0 .. 11, mode: \major, \minor or \note)::, or nil.

METHOD:: correlate
ARGUMENT:: a
ARGUMENT:: b
RETURNS:: their Pearson correlation.

METHOD:: breathing
METHOD:: sliceSeconds
METHOD:: breathe
Whether a long loop waits a millisecond every so often, inside a Routine.

METHOD:: version
A cached analysis of another version is redone.

METHOD:: maxSeconds
What link::#*analyse:: reads of a long file; 30.

METHOD:: targetRate
METHOD:: fftSize
METHOD:: hopSize
METHOD:: chromaSize
METHOD:: chromaHop
METHOD:: minBpm
METHOD:: maxBpm
METHOD:: fluxLag
METHOD:: salienceFloor
The settings. code::salienceFloor:: (150) is the least the top of an onset
curve counts as, so a pad's drift is not read as attacks.

METHOD:: majorProfile
METHOD:: minorProfile
METHOD:: noteNames
The key profiles, and the names of the pitch classes.
